\description{
Re-implementation of Stata's \code{tabulate} (short form \code{tab}), covering
\verb{tabulate oneway}, \verb{tabulate twoway} and \verb{tabulate, summarize()}.
Options have the same names as in Stata and are set as logical arguments,
e.g. \code{row = TRUE} instead of \verb{, row}.
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item Missing values are shown as categories by default, i.e. \code{s.tab(df, x)}
corresponds to Stata's \verb{tabulate x, missing}. \code{missing = FALSE} gives
Stata's default.
\item Weights. \verb{[fweight = w]} becomes \code{wt = w} (together with \code{weight_type}).
\item \verb{by x:} becomes \code{group_by(x)} before the call.
\item \code{if}/\verb{in} are expressed with \code{dplyr::filter()} or \code{dplyr::slice()}.
\item \code{generate()} is provided as a separate function, \code{\link[=s.tabgen]{s.tabgen()}}, because
tidyverse functions do not modify the data as a side effect.
}
}
\section{Addons beyond Stata}{

\code{entropy = TRUE} is an extension of statsalike and has no counterpart in
Stata's \code{tabulate}. The printed and exported output shows the entropy
values without marking them as an addon, so results that use this option
cannot be reproduced with Stata itself.
}

